<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  =============================================================================
  The Aquarius Session, running on labwc
  =============================================================================
  (An XML comment may not contain two hyphens in a row, which is why the rules
  in this file are drawn with = signs instead of the dashes used everywhere
  else in the project. It is a quirk of XML, not a change of style.)

  This is one of TWO compositor configurations in this folder. The other is
  ../niri/config.kdl. ADR 0001 left the choice open on purpose:

    labwc : a plain stacking compositor. Windows overlap, like every desktop
            you have used. Boring on purpose. Budgie 10.10 ships its entire
            desktop environment on it, which is the strongest possible evidence
            that a shell like ours can live here.
    niri  : a scrollable-tiling compositor, more opinionated and more
            interesting for the handheld idea.

  The bench run decides. Switch with one word:
      echo niri > ~/.config/aquarius-session/compositor

  =============================================================================
  WHY THIS FILE IS SO SHORT
  =============================================================================
  <default /> below loads labwc's own key bindings: a terminal on Super+Return,
  Alt+Tab to switch windows, Alt+F4 to close, Super+a to maximise, Super+arrows
  to snap. Those are good and we have no reason to reinvent them, so this file
  adds exactly two bindings on top and otherwise gets out of the way.

  =============================================================================
  TWO RULES THIS FILE OBEYS
  =============================================================================
  1. NO COLOURS IN THIS FILE. Colour in this project lives in theme/Ice.qml and
     theme/Midnight.qml, and there is exactly ONE other file allowed to hold a
     colour value: `themerc-override` next to this one, which is how labwc's own
     menu and title bars get the Aquarius look. It is an exception because it has
     to be — labwc is a C program reading a flat text file and cannot import QML,
     so somebody has to write the numbers out twice. Every value in it is copied
     digit for digit from theme/Ice.qml and tests/test-shell.sh section 15b fails
     the build if one of them is not in Ice. Nothing else here, this file
     included, may name a colour; section 15 still enforces that.

     What this file DOES carry is the FONT for those same surfaces (the <theme>
     section below), because a font is not a colour and there is no themerc key
     for it — labwc takes fonts from rc.xml and colours from the themerc.

  2. NO PATHS. The shell is started from the autostart file next to this one,
     with a bare `qs`, because ../aquarius-session exports QS_CONFIG_PATH first.

  =============================================================================
  KEY BINDINGS ADDED BY US
  =============================================================================
    W-space     open or close the Aquarius search palette (the shell's own IPC)
    W-Return    a terminal (ptyxis, the one AquariusOS ships)
    W-Tab       open the Aquarius app switcher and step forward through it
    W-S-Tab     the same, backwards
    W-grave     cycle the windows of the app you are in, with no panel
    A-Tab       the app switcher again, for the Windows keyboard style
    A-S-Tab     the same, backwards
    W-S-e       leave the session and return to the login screen

  "W" is labwc's letter for Super (the Windows/Command key). Its other
  modifiers are S=shift, C=control, A=alt.

  =============================================================================
  THE DESKTOP RIGHT-CLICK MENU
  =============================================================================
  Right-clicking the empty desktop opens the Aquarius menu (Search, System
  Settings, Change Wallpaper, and the session actions). The menu's CONTENTS are
  in menu.xml next to this file; the <mouse> section below is only what binds a
  right-click on the root to it.

  <default /> inside <mouse> loads labwc's built-in mouse bindings — window
  dragging, edge-resize, focus-on-click, alt-drag to move — so this section adds
  the root menu WITHOUT throwing those away. Left-clicking the root is left
  alone, so the desktop stays inert to a left click, which is what a clean
  desktop with no icons should do.

  The menu's LOOK is neither of those files: it is `themerc-override`, also
  next to this one, which is the only place in this project outside theme/ that
  is allowed to hold a colour. Read its header before touching it.

  Format reference: labwc-config(5), labwc-actions(5), labwc-menu(5) and
  labwc-theme(5), all as of labwc 0.20.2.
  =============================================================================
-->

<labwc_config>

  <core>
    <!-- Let the compositor draw title bars rather than each application
         drawing its own. Fewer competing visual languages on screen. -->
    <decoration>server</decoration>
    <gap>8</gap>
  </core>

  <!--
    =========================================================================
    THE FONT labwc DRAWS ITS OWN SURFACES IN
    =========================================================================
    The COLOURS of the desktop menu and the window title bars are in
    `themerc-override` next to this file. Their FONT is not: labwc takes theme
    colours from a themerc and fonts from rc.xml, so the two halves of one look
    live in two files. That is labwc's split, not ours.

    Inter is the shell's own UI face — theme/Theme.qml picks it as `fontBody`,
    and every label in the bar, the dock, the menus and the panels is drawn in
    it. Without these four lines labwc would draw its menu in "sans", whatever
    that resolves to on the machine, and the desktop menu would be in a
    different typeface from the shell menu two inches above it.

    IF INTER IS NOT INSTALLED labwc falls back to its default on its own, which
    is the same outcome the shell's Theme.pickFont() arranges for itself. The OS
    image installs Sora, Inter and JetBrains Mono; a plain Fedora clone may not
    have them, and neither half breaks when it does not.

    THE SIZES ARE THE SHELL'S, and labwc's <size> is in PIXELS
    (labwc-config(5): "Font size in pixels. Default is 10."), which is the same
    unit theme/Theme.qml uses — it sets font.pixelSize, never pointSize. So:

      MenuItem   19  = Theme.fsBody     — one row of the Aquarius menu
      MenuHeader 15  = Theme.fsCaption  — a menu title, quieter than its items
      ActiveWindow / InactiveWindow
                 15  = Theme.fsCaption  — the size the top bar draws its own
                                          labels at, and a title bar is the same
                                          kind of chrome as the bar

    ⚠️ AQ_UI_SCALE DOES NOT REACH HERE. The shell multiplies every size by that
    variable; labwc knows nothing about it. Set it and the shell grows while
    these do not. Written up as a follow-up in themerc-override's header,
    alongside the Midnight problem, because both want the same fix.

    Attribute ORDER matters in labwc when a font is written on one line —
    labwc-config(5) is explicit that `place` must come before `name` and `size`,
    or the setting lands on every place at once. Child elements have no such
    trap, so these are written as children.
  -->
  <theme>

    <font place="MenuItem">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>19</size>
    </font>

    <font place="MenuHeader">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>15</size>
    </font>

    <font place="ActiveWindow">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>15</size>
    </font>

    <font place="InactiveWindow">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>15</size>
    </font>

  </theme>

  <keyboard>

    <!-- labwc's own defaults: Super+Return terminal, Alt+Tab, Alt+F4,
         Super+a maximize, Super+arrows snap, plus the volume and brightness
         keys. Keeping them means this file only has to describe what is
         different about Aquarius. -->
    <default />

    <!--
      The Aquarius search palette.

      This does not launch a program. It sends a message to the shell that is
      ALREADY running, telling it to show or hide its search box.
      `qs ipc call <target> <function> [arguments]` is Quickshell's own
      mechanism for that. The target "search" and its function "toggle" are
      declared by the IpcHandler in components/search/FlowSearch.qml.

      Which shell instance gets the message is not an argument: `qs ipc`
      picks the instance running the config named by QS_CONFIG_PATH, which
      labwc inherited from the launcher. (Checked against the help text of
      `qs ipc` on Quickshell 0.2.1, 2026-09-01. An earlier version of this line passed
      "aquarius-shell" as a first positional, which `qs` would have read as
      the target name — and no such target exists.)

      If the palette does not open, ask the running shell what it offers:
          qs ipc show
    -->
    <keybind key="W-space">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call search toggle" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      A terminal on Super+Return.

      <default /> above already binds Super+Return to a terminal — labwc's own
      default, which reaches for xterm/foot/alacritty in turn. AquariusOS ships
      ptyxis, so this names it and the key opens the terminal the OS actually
      has.

      ⚠️ THIS LINE EXISTS TO MAKE THE TWO COPIES OF THIS FILE IDENTICAL. What an
      installed machine reads is system_files/usr/share/aquarius/labwc/rc.xml in
      the os-image repo, and that copy already had this keybind. This one did
      not, which meant the two files differed in a way that was not a comment —
      and once that is true, "diff them and read the prose" stops being a way to
      check they agree. The shell's rc.xml is deliberately the SUPERSET, so the
      two can be kept content-identical apart from the comments.

      On a plain Fedora clone with no ptyxis installed the keybind is simply
      inert: labwc runs the command, the command does not exist, and nothing
      happens. It costs one key, not a broken session.
    -->
    <keybind key="W-Return">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>ptyxis</command>
      </action>
    </keybind>

    <!-- ====================================================================== -->
    <!-- BEGIN: the Aquarius app switcher. Added 2026-09-06.                     -->
    <!-- ====================================================================== -->
    <!--
      THE ONLY BLOCK IN THIS FILE THE APP SWITCHER OWNS. Everything it needs is
      between this banner and the matching END banner further down, including
      the one setting outside <keyboard>. Nothing else in the file was touched.

      ======================================================================
      WHAT THESE FIVE BINDINGS DO
      ======================================================================
      None of them launches a program. Each one sends a short message to the
      shell that is ALREADY running, telling it to show its app switcher and
      move the selection. `qs ipc call <target> <function>` is Quickshell's own
      mechanism for that; the target "switcher" and its functions are declared
      by the IpcHandler in components/switcher/AppSwitcher.qml.

      If a key seems to do nothing, ask the running shell what it offers:
          qs ipc show

      ======================================================================
      WHY BOTH SUPER AND ALT ARE BOUND, ALL THE TIME
      ======================================================================
      AquariusOS asks "Mac or Windows?" once, at first login, and that answer
      decides which physical key is which:

        Mac style      Aquarius Keys swaps the two keys beside the space bar, so
                       the key a Mac user reaches for with their thumb sends
                       SUPER. Command+Tab arrives here as W-Tab. (The
                       Windows-logo key becomes Option and sends Alt.)
        Windows style  nothing is swapped and the remapper does not even run.
                       Alt+Tab arrives here as A-Tab.

      Binding both means this file never has to change when somebody runs
      `aq keys mac` or `aq keys windows`. What DOES change is what the panel
      lists — applications in the Mac style, windows in the Windows style — and
      the shell reads that from ~/.config/aquarius/keys.conf itself, live, with
      no restart. One question, asked once; no second setting anywhere.

      ======================================================================
      WHY labwc's OWN SWITCHER IS NOT USED ANY MORE
      ======================================================================
      These bindings used to be NextWindow and PreviousWindow, which are
      labwc's own actions and draw labwc's own on-screen list. That list works,
      but it is a plain list of window titles in labwc's theme, and it cannot
      group five windows of one editor into one application the way a Mac does.
      The Aquarius switcher is drawn by the shell, from the same icon theme and
      the same colours as the dock, and it knows about applications.

      labwc's list is switched off below, outside </keyboard>, so that nothing
      can put two switchers on screen at once.

      ======================================================================
      HOW IT KNOWS YOU LET GO OF THE KEY
      ======================================================================
      It is not in this file, and that surprises people, so: the shell sees the
      release ITSELF. Its panel asks the compositor for the keyboard while it is
      on screen, and because the Super key on its own is bound to nothing here,
      labwc passes the release of it straight through to whoever has the
      keyboard — which by then is the panel. The whole explanation, and the two
      approaches that were tried first, are in the aquarius-shell repository at
      docs/app-switcher.md.

      ⚠️ DO NOT ADD A BINDING FOR Super_L OR Alt_L HERE. Binding the modifier on
      its own would make labwc swallow its press, and a swallowed press means a
      swallowed release — which is the one event this whole feature depends on.
    -->

    <!-- Mac keyboard style: Command+Tab and Command+Shift+Tab. -->
    <keybind key="W-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher next" />
    </keybind>

    <keybind key="W-S-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher prev" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      Command+` — step through the windows of the app you are already in,
      without putting anything on screen. The key to the left of the 1 on a US
      keyboard; labwc's name for it is "grave".
    -->
    <keybind key="W-grave">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher cycle" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      Windows keyboard style: Alt+Tab and Alt+Shift+Tab.

      These REPLACE labwc's built-in Alt+Tab. When two keybinds name the same
      key, labwc keeps the last one written down and drops the earlier — so
      these lines, coming after <default /> at the top of this section, are the
      ones that win. (Checked in labwc 0.20.2's own source:
      deduplicate_key_bindings() in src/config/rcxml.c.)
    -->
    <keybind key="A-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher next" />
    </keybind>

    <keybind key="A-S-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher prev" />
    </keybind>

    <!-- Leave the session and go back to the login screen. -->
    <keybind key="W-S-e">
      <action name="Exit" />
    </keybind>

  </keyboard>

  <!--
    labwc's own window switcher, switched off.

    The five bindings above no longer use NextWindow or PreviousWindow, so this
    list should never appear on its own. This turns it off anyway, belt and
    braces: if some future binding or a stray default ever reaches those actions
    again, the result would be TWO switchers on screen at the same time, and
    that is a confusing thing to debug from a screenshot.

    `show="no"` on the <osd> element is labwc 0.20's spelling for it — checked
    against labwc-config(5) at 0.20.2, the version AquariusOS builds. There is
    no `<windowSwitcher show="">` attribute; the attribute belongs to <osd>.
  -->
  <windowSwitcher>
    <osd show="no" />
  </windowSwitcher>
  <!-- ====================================================================== -->
  <!-- END: the Aquarius app switcher                                          -->
  <!-- ====================================================================== -->

  <mouse>

    <!-- labwc's own mouse bindings: dragging a title bar, edge-resize, click to
         focus and raise, alt-drag to move a window. Keeping them means this
         section only has to describe what is different about Aquarius. -->
    <default />

    <!-- Right-click the empty desktop -> the Aquarius menu (menu.xml). Left
         click on the root is deliberately left unbound, so a clean desktop with
         no icons does nothing on a left click. -->
    <context name="Root">
      <mousebind button="Right" action="Press">
        <action name="ShowMenu" menu="root-menu" />
      </mousebind>
    </context>

  </mouse>

</labwc_config>
